# Subjective Career Success and Emotional Well-Being: Longitudinal Predictive Power of Selection, Optimization, and Compensation.

**Authors:** Wiese, Bettina S., Freund, Alexandra M., Baltes, Paul B.

## Abstract
A 3-year study of 82 young professionals found that work-related well-being was predicted by selection (commitment to personal goals), optimization (application of goal-related skills), and compensation (maintaining goals in the face of loss). The degree of compensation predicted emotional well-being and job satisfaction 3 years later. (Contains 56 references.) (SK)

**Source:** https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ646677

